WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Laughter]  emmanuel musili owns this restaurant in  kitui county in eastern kenya but his  gas banners have not been used for a  while he tried to use gas for cooking  but the cost has been rising globally  musili says his expenses were too high  so he switched to charcoal  of course we care about the environment  but as times goes to be  more harder it kind becomes a time that  you don't mind about  the environment but to take care of the  cost production  here is a problem musile is not the only  one who has decided to put aside his gas  cylinder the demand for charcoal is  growing the government has reduced the  cost of gas but many people we've talked  to here say they still cannot afford it  for cooking they say they use charcoal  instead because it is cheaper  kenya's charcoal trade is worth nearly  half a billion dollars and employs  thousands of people but it continues to  threaten the survival of trees and water  catchment areas this is despite the fact  charcoal burning is restricted  this is a protected forest in kitui  south reserve  it's vast and some of the trees have  been here for many years sacks of  charcoal are transported to different  markets daily  i used to work in mombasa but because of  corona i lost my job  that is how i decided to burn charcoal i  have needs and life has to go on the  business is my livelihood i feed my  children and take care of my family  officials whose work it is to protect  forests try to replant what is destroyed  and talk to communities about how they  can harvest or prune trees sustainably  we are way way below they recommended  the international levels of 10 percent  we are 8.3 percent countrywide which of  course is less than the recommended  level  in the world which is ten percent  musili says he wants to protect the  environment but his priority is keeping  his restaurant afloat and earning are  leaving catherine soy al jazeera ketui  and easton kenya

